  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Validating Momala malaria mobile diagnostics in Kenya</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">More than 500,000 people die of Malaria in Africa each year, despite it being a preventable and treatable disease. Healthcare providers, in particular in rural areas, often lack sufficient qualified care workers to perform adequate diagnostics, leading to limited capacity in hospitals, poor quality of diagnostics and diagnoses and unnecessary high costs. As a result, many patients without malaria diagnoses are over-treated with medication anyway which in turn could lead to mutated malaria parasites becoming resistant to the medicines available.Dutch e-health company Momala BV has developed a mobile app using an algorithm to detect and diagnose malaria in less than 5 minutes based on microscopic images of blood tests searching for relevant parasite (plasmodium) varieties. This technology can prevent misdiagnoses, reduce overtreatment from 50% to 10% and provide faster diagnoses at considerably lower costs compared to commonly used Rapid Diagnostics Tests. To introduce the Momala technology in Kenya and to prepare for future investments, Technical Assistance provided support to Momala to validate the market and to further develop the technology based on test results. The project included a field pilot (dry-run), training local staff and partners, testing of several pricing and licensing models, setting-up a legal entity in Kenia, establishing local diagnostics partnerships, preparing for local medical and CE certification and WHO pre-qualification and IP measures and developing a CSR policy.Unfortunately, Momala  seized its activities entirely. The project did not result in further investments.</narrative>
  </description>
